WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.767
Your agent now knows the product.

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:06.133
Now meet a team that
arrives already working.

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:08.367
Orbit can research an
account while you sleep,

00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:11.267
and it still can't send a word.

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:16.000
In this clip you'll know each
pack and one real trigger from it.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.733
You'll also know the limits no agent
can raise, and how to switch one on.

00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:24.667
An agent that wakes on its own is useful.

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:27.933
One that also approves its
own work is a liability,

00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:31.733
and Mia finds out from a
customer, not from a log.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.700
Here, agents run on triggers,
inside limits they cannot raise.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.733
They ship in packs.

00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:38.833
A pack is a package that carries

00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:41.900
agents together with
their triggers and skills.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.233
The packs are core, CRM,
data, growth, ops and revenue.

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:49.533
A trigger is what wakes an agent:
an event, a schedule, or you.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:52.133
First, one trigger from each pack.

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:56.167
Core: the SDR agent wakes on a verified
buying signal, a funding round,

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:59.067
a hiring spike or an intent surge.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:02.933
It researches the account,
drafts one personal first touch,

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:05.400
and proposes the send. It never sends.

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:09.700
CRM: the meeting prep agent
wakes when a meeting is booked.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.633
It writes a brief with who is
coming, the deal, open items,

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:17.500
signals and talking points,
each line tied to a record.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:21.800
Data: the enrichment agent
runs weekly, early on Monday.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.467
It finds empty or stale fields
worth filling, quotes the cost,

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.500
and proposes the run. It
never spends on its own.

00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:32.400
Growth: the SEO and GEO
watch agent runs daily.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.167
It looks for decaying pages and gaps
in AI answers, drafts the top refresh,

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:40.967
and proposes publishing it
through the claims gate.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.933
Ops: the ops copilot runs
when you ask what's going on.

00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:49.533
It answers across engines,
approvals, agents and spend,

00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:54.300
with every figure traced to an
operation. It suggests, and never acts.

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:58.433
Revenue: the reply triage agent
wakes when a reply arrives.

00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:02.867
It drafts the answer to a
meeting request or an objection,

00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:06.400
and hands anything sensitive
to the account owner.

00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:10.267
Here's what keeps it calm. An
event trigger skips bulk writers,

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:15.333
so a big import or a C R M sync
doesn't wake an agent for every row.

00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:17.600
Each trigger is also capped per hour,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.233
and an over cap fire
is recorded as skipped.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:24.367
Second, the limits, because
they're the point. An agent asks.

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.567
A person decides. It can
never approve its own work.

00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:30.267
Approving, rejecting, executing, signing,

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:34.267
creating keys and switching
Dry Mode are human only.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:37.767
An agent that tries is refused,
and told which screen to use.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.933
An agent can't widen
its own rights either.

00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:44.967
It can't grant itself scopes,
mint keys or loosen a policy.

00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:47.900
And any operation that spends money inside

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.867
itself is never handed to an agent skill.

00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:54.867
Now the rule that surprises people.
Mia creates an agent key for Orbit.

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:58.433
Everything Orbit proposes is
attributed to Orbit and to Mia,

00:02:58.433 --> 00:03:01.100
so Mia can't approve, reject
or execute it. Sophia can.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.967
Its authority is also capped
three ways, by the key's scopes,

00:03:04.967 --> 00:03:07.300
the agent's scopes and the
creator's current role.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.233
Whichever is narrowest wins.

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:12.700
Third, switch one on. In the
console: Settings, then Agents.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.733
Each pack card shows its
state: not installed, off, on,

00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:17.600
or waiting for approval.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:21.367
Choose Install pack, or Turn on
for your default environment.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:25.133
That's a request, and a second
owner or admin approves it.

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:28.900
Or ask your agent: "Turn on
the crm-agents pack in dev".

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.400
The agent proposes, reports
the approval number, and waits.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.433
Approval registers each
agent, publishes its skills,

00:03:36.433 --> 00:03:38.433
and sets up its triggers.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.933
Fourth, scope them, on the same page.

00:03:40.933 --> 00:03:43.967
Each agent lists its
triggers, running or paused,

00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:47.267
and its hard limits on
steps, cost and time.

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:50.833
You can edit its scopes
down, stop it, or revive it.

00:03:50.833 --> 00:03:54.700
Or ask your agent: "Show my
agents and what triggers them".

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:58.867
Then watch them. Each agent
shows its last thirty days: runs,

00:03:58.867 --> 00:04:01.133
proposals approved against
proposed, and cost.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:04.167
For the step by step trace of
any run, open Operate, then Runs.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:07.267
Or ask your agent: "How did
my agents do this month".

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:10.267
Every figure comes with
its source operation.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.100
One habit worth copying:

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.633
start with the ops copilot
and the meeting prep agent.

00:04:15.633 --> 00:04:19.300
They suggest and brief, so
nothing leaves the building.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.200
Add the SDR agent once the drafts
you read in Dry Mode look right.

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:24.700
One honest note.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.333
Agents that depend on engines GTM
OS hasn't built yet are not shipped,

00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:31.400
so this clip doesn't show them.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.033
Everything here runs on
engines that exist today.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.233
Here's the doubt: an agent that wakes
by itself will spam my customers.

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:42.933
It can't send. A send is a proposal
a different person approves.

00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:44.867
Triggers are capped per hour,

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:46.933
and a first message to someone nobody

00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:49.367
has written to always waits for a person.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.833
Quick pass: Packs, each with its
trigger. Limits no agent can raise.

00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:59.533
And a switch that's a proposal
a second person approves.

00:04:59.567 --> 00:05:05.033
You now have a team that proposes
work, inside limits it can't raise,

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:07.367
with a person on every decision.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:11.800
Next up, Tutorial: your first
week, a checkpoint a day.
